{"faiths":[{"id":"catholic","n":"Catholic Christianity","fam":"christian","c":"#d6ac34","d":"Latin Christianity of the western Roman Church, led by the Pope. Before the Great Schism of 1054 this marks the Latin-speaking West.","w":"Catholic Church"},{"id":"orthodox","n":"Eastern Orthodox Christianity","fam":"christian","c":"#8b3d8f","d":"Greek-rite Christianity of Byzantium and its daughter churches (Bulgaria, Serbia, Rus, Georgia, Romania). Before 1054 this marks the Greek-speaking East.","w":"Eastern Orthodox Church"},{"id":"protestant","n":"Protestant Christianity","fam":"christian","c":"#3f6fb8","d":"Churches of the Reformation (Lutheran, Reformed/Calvinist, Anglican and their offshoots), from 1517. Bohemia c. 1420–1620 is shown here for the Hussite (Utraquist) movement that anticipated it.","w":"Protestantism"},{"id":"lds","n":"Latter-day Saints (Mormon)","fam":"christian","c":"#5b8f9e","d":"The Restorationist church founded by Joseph Smith (1830); the Mormon exodus under Brigham Young settled the Great Basin from 1847, and Utah has had a Latter-day Saint majority ever since.","w":"The Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-day Saints"},{"id":"oriental","n":"Oriental Orthodox Christianity","fam":"christian","c":"#c25a3c","d":"The churches that rejected the Council of Chalcedon (451): Coptic, Ethiopian, Armenian and Syriac Orthodox.","w":"Oriental Orthodox Churches"},{"id":"nestorian","n":"Church of the East","fam":"christian","c":"#8e6c3a","d":"The Syriac \"Nestorian\" church of the Persian Empire, which spread along the Silk Road to Central Asia, India and China.","w":"Church of the East"},{"id":"sunni","n":"Sunni Islam","fam":"islam","c":"#3e8b46","d":"The majority branch of Islam, founded on the Quran and the Sunna of the Prophet Muhammad (d. 632).","w":"Sunni Islam"},{"id":"shia","n":"Shia Islam","fam":"islam","c":"#1e5e48","d":"The branch of Islam that follows the line of Ali and the Imams; includes Twelvers (Iran, Iraq, Azerbaijan, Lebanon), Zaydis (Yemen) and Alawites.","w":"Shia Islam"},{"id":"ibadi","n":"Ibadi Islam","fam":"islam","c":"#86b35c","d":"The surviving branch of the early Kharijite movement, dominant in Oman and once in parts of the Maghreb.","w":"Ibadi Islam"},{"id":"judaism","n":"Judaism","fam":"jewish","c":"#5fb3dd","d":"The religion of the Israelites and the Jewish people. Shown as a majority faith in ancient Israel and Judah, Himyar (c. 380–525) and modern Israel.","w":"Judaism"},{"id":"hindu","n":"Hinduism","fam":"indian","c":"#e2762c","d":"The family of Indian traditions grown from Vedic religion (Shaiva, Vaishnava, Shakta). In Southeast Asia before c. 1300 it also marks the Hindu-Buddhist court religions of Funan, Champa, Angkor and Java.","w":"Hinduism"},{"id":"vedic","n":"Vedic religion","fam":"indian","c":"#c99449","d":"The religion of the Vedas brought by Indo-Aryan speakers to the Punjab and Ganges plain (c. 1500–500 BCE), ancestor of Hinduism.","w":"Historical Vedic religion"},{"id":"harappan","n":"Harappan religion","fam":"indian","c":"#a8714a","d":"The little-understood religion of the Indus Valley cities (c. 3300–1800 BCE): ritual bathing, fertility figures and seals with animal and \"proto-Shiva\" motifs.","w":"Indus Valley Civilisation"},{"id":"theravada","n":"Theravada Buddhism","fam":"indian","c":"#efd57c","d":"The \"Way of the Elders\", the Pali-canon Buddhism of Sri Lanka, Burma, Thailand, Laos and Cambodia.","w":"Theravada"},{"id":"mahayana","n":"Mahayana Buddhism","fam":"indian","c":"#e9b98a","d":"The \"Great Vehicle\" Buddhism of Gandhara, Central Asia, Srivijaya, the Palas and Korea; in China and Vietnam it blended into the Chinese tradition.","w":"Mahayana"},{"id":"vajrayana","n":"Tibetan Buddhism","fam":"indian","c":"#b98ad2","d":"Vajrayana Buddhism of Tibet, Bhutan and Ladakh, adopted by the Mongols (1570s) and Kalmyks.","w":"Tibetan Buddhism"},{"id":"sikh","n":"Sikhism","fam":"indian","c":"#2a95b8","d":"Founded by Guru Nanak (1469–1539) in the Punjab; the majority faith of Indian Punjab since 1966.","w":"Sikhism"},{"id":"east_asian","n":"Chinese traditional religion","fam":"eastasian","c":"#bc3a33","d":"The Chinese blend of ancestor veneration, Confucianism, Taoism and Mahayana Buddhism, shared with Vietnam and (after 1392) Neo-Confucian Korea.","w":"Chinese folk religion"},{"id":"shinto","n":"Shinto and Japanese Buddhism","fam":"eastasian","c":"#e2848b","d":"Worship of the kami, blended with Buddhism from the 6th century into the Japanese tradition.","w":"Shinto"},{"id":"zoroastrian","n":"Zoroastrianism","fam":"iranian","c":"#d4664a","d":"The faith of the prophet Zarathustra and Ahura Mazda; state religion of the Achaemenid, Parthian and Sasanian empires.","w":"Zoroastrianism"},{"id":"manichaean","n":"Manichaeism","fam":"iranian","c":"#7d6aa9","d":"The dualist faith of the prophet Mani (d. 274); state religion of the Uyghur Khaganate (763–840).","w":"Manichaeism"},{"id":"iranian","n":"Early Indo-Iranian religion","fam":"iranian","c":"#b8885a","d":"The pre-Zoroastrian religion of the Iranian peoples, shared roots with the Vedic gods.","w":"Proto-Indo-Iranian religion"},{"id":"egyptian","n":"Ancient Egyptian religion","fam":"ancient","c":"#cfb16a","d":"The religion of Pharaonic Egypt and Kush: Ra, Osiris, Isis, Amun and the divine king. It faded under Christianity after c. 350 CE.","w":"Ancient Egyptian religion"},{"id":"mesopotamian","n":"Mesopotamian religion","fam":"ancient","c":"#a5805a","d":"The temple religion of Sumer, Akkad, Babylon, Assyria and Elam: Anu, Enlil, Inanna/Ishtar, Marduk, Ashur.","w":"Ancient Mesopotamian religion"},{"id":"canaanite","n":"Canaanite and Punic religion","fam":"ancient","c":"#94704f","d":"The religion of the Canaanites, Phoenicians, Aramaeans and Carthaginians: El, Baal, Astarte, Melqart, Tanit.","w":"Ancient Canaanite religion"},{"id":"anatolian","n":"Anatolian religion","fam":"ancient","c":"#a99070","d":"The Hittite, Luwian, Phrygian and Lydian religions of Asia Minor (\"the thousand gods of Hatti\", Cybele).","w":"Hittite mythology and religion"},{"id":"arabian","n":"Arabian polytheism","fam":"ancient","c":"#c99e6c","d":"The gods of pre-Islamic Arabia (Hubal, al-Lat, al-Uzza, Manat) and the South Arabian kingdoms (Almaqah).","w":"Pre-Islamic Arabia"},{"id":"hellenic","n":"Greek and Roman religion","fam":"ancient","c":"#6d9fc6","d":"The Olympian gods of Greece and Rome and the syncretic cults of the Hellenistic and Roman world, until Christianity replaced them (4th century CE).","w":"Religion in ancient Rome"},{"id":"old_european","n":"Bronze Age European religion","fam":"europagan","c":"#8d8f78","d":"The little-known religions of Neolithic and Bronze Age Europe: megaliths, sun discs and sky gods.","w":"Prehistoric religion"},{"id":"celtic","n":"Celtic religion","fam":"europagan","c":"#5d9a6b","d":"The druidic religion of the Gauls, Britons and Irish: Lugus, Taranis, Brigid and sacred groves.","w":"Ancient Celtic religion"},{"id":"iberian","n":"Iberian and Celtiberian religion","fam":"europagan","c":"#82995a","d":"The pre-Roman religions of the Iberian peninsula.","w":"Iberians"},{"id":"germanic","n":"Germanic and Norse paganism","fam":"europagan","c":"#6c80a8","d":"The religion of the Germanic peoples and the Vikings: Odin/Woden, Thor, Freyr and the sacred groves of Uppsala.","w":"Germanic paganism"},{"id":"slavic","n":"Slavic paganism","fam":"europagan","c":"#93a75c","d":"The religion of the early Slavs: Perun, Veles, Svarog and Mokosh.","w":"Slavic paganism"},{"id":"baltic","n":"Baltic paganism","fam":"europagan","c":"#6f9d8d","d":"The religion of the Prussians, Latvians and Lithuanians (Perkūnas, Dievas). Lithuania was the last pagan state in Europe (to 1387).","w":"Baltic religion"},{"id":"thracian","n":"Thracian and Dacian religion","fam":"europagan","c":"#9c7ea3","d":"The religion of the Thracians and Dacians: the horseman hero, Zalmoxis and Orphic cults.","w":"Thracian religion"},{"id":"tengri","n":"Tengrism and steppe religion","fam":"steppe","c":"#4fb1a